            <ArticleTitle>Metatarsalgia caused by an increase in circulating platelets: a case report.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>216-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A patient with a year-long history of metatarsalgia was found to have an elevated platelet count due to essential thrombocythemia, a benign myeloproliferative disease. Reduction of the platelet count with chemotherapy eliminated the foot pain. The authors recommended that a platelet count be part of the work-up of patients with metatarsalgia when the etiology of the foot pain is unclear.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
